1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,250
I want to build this man myself, not take
one off a shelf.

2
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:00,500
I would rather build this man myself than
accept somebody else.

3
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:00,750
The town is already what it is. Nothing
here wants changing by me.

4
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:01,000
Yes. I have never got anywhere in my life
by standing at a door.

5
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:01,250
First I want to know what I am allowed to
be. Then I will spend it.

6
00:00:01,250 --> 00:00:01,500
A man with no limits is a daydream. Give
me a budget and I will keep it.

7
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:01,750
Six for the body, none for the temper, two
for the trade. That is honest.

8
00:00:01,750 --> 00:00:02,000
Now: how did I come to be here at all?
There is only one true answer.

9
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:02,250
I have no patience for scrolling. I know
the word I am looking for.

10
00:00:02,250 --> 00:00:02,500
I did not fight my way out of anything.

11
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:02,750
I was asleep with the fan on, in the
middle of the afternoon.

12
00:00:02,750 --> 00:00:03,000
No shelter. No convoy. No warning that I
was awake to hear.

13
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:03,250
Spell it out plainly and it stops being
shameful. It is just what happened.

14
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:03,500
Missed. It has the decency to be accurate,
which is more than I manage.

15
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:03,750
Thirty-four years of it. There is no
question what I put down here.

16
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:04,000
I know the word. I wore it on a hat for
most of my life.

17
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:04,250
Mail.

18
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:04,500
Three routes, all of them inside two miles
of this table.

19
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:04,750
Neither snow nor rain, they told us, and
we believed it enough to walk.

20
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:05,000
That is the whole of what I am qualified
for.

21
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:05,250
One entry. I never was a man of many
options.

22
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:05,500
Mail carrier. A hat, a bag, a whistle, and
the watch on my wrist.

23
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:05,750
The schooling and the licence I will take
as they are. They are true enough.

24
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:06,000
Now the body I actually have, and not the
one I would have ordered.

25
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:06,250
I am not strong. Wiry is the kind word. I
would rather be honest than flattered.

26
00:00:06,250 --> 00:00:06,500
Next, the hands.

27
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:06,750
Thirty-four winters of sorting. The
fingers are stiff and clumsy now.

28
00:00:06,750 --> 00:00:07,000
And then the part of me that has not worn
out.

29
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:07,250
I remember every name on three routes.
That is not nothing.

30
00:00:07,250 --> 00:00:07,500
And I can still do arithmetic on my feet,
which today will matter.

31
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:07,750
Last, the eyes. This is the only part of
me I have actually earned.

32
00:00:07,750 --> 00:00:08,000
Thirty-four years of being paid to see
whether a house was right.

33
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:08,250
A door open a hand width when it was shut
yesterday. I see that.

34
00:00:08,250 --> 00:00:08,500
A shape in a yard that is the wrong shape
for a yard.

35
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:08,750
Seeing a thing before it sees me is the
only fight I am likely to win.

36
00:00:08,750 --> 00:00:09,000
I am not going to buy strength I do not
have just because there is room.

37
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:09,250
I will look these up by name. I know
exactly what is wrong with me.

38
00:00:09,250 --> 00:00:09,500
The knees first. They are the thing that
decides what today can be.

39
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:09,750
Pavement. Eight to eleven miles a day for
thirty-four years.

40
00:00:09,750 --> 00:00:10,000
Nobody warns you that it is the knees and
not the feet.

41
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:10,250
A short word for a long complaint.

42
00:00:10,250 --> 00:00:10,500
There it is, in a list, like a diagnosis.

43
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:10,750
Bad knees. It is not a complaint, it is a
fact with a consequence: I walk.

44
00:00:10,750 --> 00:00:11,000
And the other thing, the one that is
actually funny if you knew my trade.

45
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:11,250
Far-sighted. Fifteen years now, and
getting no better.

46
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:11,500
A man who read four thousand addresses a
week, who cannot read a label.

47
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:11,750
The glasses live in my shirt pocket, and
they are one small thing to lose.

48
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:12,000
Say it out loud and it costs less than
hiding it does.

49
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:12,250
Admitting it buys me something. I would
rather be honest and pay for it.

50
00:00:12,250 --> 00:00:12,500
Now what that admission is for. Three
things, and they are all one thing.

51
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:12,750
The first is what my wife called the most
annoying quality a man ever had.

52
00:00:12,750 --> 00:00:13,000
I sleep. Anywhere, on anything, through
anything.

53
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:13,250
Up at four in the morning for thirty-four
years will do that to a man.

54
00:00:13,250 --> 00:00:13,500
Tonight it stops being a joke and starts
being the whole of my plan.

55
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:13,750
There it is, and the knees have already
paid for it.

56
00:00:13,750 --> 00:00:14,000
A man who does not sleep makes the mistake
I cannot afford twice.

57
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:14,250
The second thing is the same thing, from
the other side.

58
00:00:14,250 --> 00:00:14,500
Hearing.

59
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:14,750
Thirty-four years of dogs teaches you
which side of a fence a thing is on.

60
00:00:14,750 --> 00:00:15,000
This town has gone very quiet, so what
sound is left is worth its weight.

61
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:15,250
I have always known this street by its
noise more than by its faces.

62
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:15,500
And the far sight has already paid for
this one too.

63
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:15,750
Good hearing has kept me out of two dog
bites and one very bad house.

64
00:00:15,750 --> 00:00:16,000
And the third: how I put my feet down.

65
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:16,250
Quietly.

66
00:00:16,250 --> 00:00:16,500
Thirty-four years of not waking the night
nurse who slept until noon.

67
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:16,750
You set a foot down the way you set down a
letter you will not crease.

68
00:00:16,750 --> 00:00:17,000
This is the last thing my bad knees and
bad eyes are going to buy me.

69
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:17,250
And then I am done bargaining with myself.

70
00:00:17,250 --> 00:00:17,500
Taken. Every good thing about me is paid
for by a bad one, exactly.

71
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:17,750
Now what I can actually do, which is a
shorter list than you would hope.

72
00:00:17,750 --> 00:00:18,000
Two things I have really got, and I will
spend both on them.

73
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:18,250
Walking.

74
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:18,500
Eight to eleven miles a day, in February,
uphill on Torrey Street.

75
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:18,750
Not fast. Never fast. But all day, and the
distances here are mine.

76
00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:19,000
It is the only athletic thing about me and
I earned every mile.

77
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:19,250
There.

78
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:19,500
Walking is the only equipment I own that
has never once failed me.

79
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:19,750
And the other one the post office paid
for, after Kowalski went down.

80
00:00:19,750 --> 00:00:20,000
A man dropped in a driveway on my east
route, in August, and I was first there.

81
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:20,250
They sent us all on a course after that. I
did not resent it.

82
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:20,500
I know what to do about bleeding.

83
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:20,750
And I know what not to do about a broken
bone, which matters more.

84
00:00:20,750 --> 00:00:21,000
That did nothing. The page is exactly as I
left it.

85
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:21,250
Walking is the only equipment I own that
has never once failed me.

86
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:21,500
I am fumbling at this. Let me clear it and
start the word again.

87
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:21,750
The other thing I have is a card in my
wallet from a course.

88
00:00:21,750 --> 00:00:22,000
Kowalski went down in a driveway on the
east route, in August.

89
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:22,250
I got there first and I did the right
things in the right order.

90
00:00:22,250 --> 00:00:22,500
They paid for the training after that,
which was decent of them.

91
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:22,750
Let me see it written down.

92
00:00:22,750 --> 00:00:23,000
Bleeding, and what not to do about a
broken bone. That is what I know.

93
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:23,250
Now let me read the whole of it over
before I open any door.

94
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:23,500
Twenty-five, it says. I have shoes older
than that.

95
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:23,750
Sixty.

96
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:24,000
Sixty-one, since March. My sister sent a
card and I still have it.

97
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:24,250
That is the man who has to walk to Elm
today. No point pretending otherwise.

98
00:00:24,250 --> 00:00:24,500
It says random. I have had one name my
whole life and I will use it.

99
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:24,750
A, for Ambrose, which was my grandfather
on my mother side.

100
00:00:24,750 --> 00:00:25,000
Nobody has called me that since my mother
died.

101
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:25,250
The men on the routes called me Bram and
so did my wife.

102
00:00:25,250 --> 00:00:25,500
But a name on a form is the long one, and
this feels like a form.

103
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:25,750
Ambrose Halloran, third of four, born on
the wrong side of the tracks.

104
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:26,000
The side the tracks were actually built
for, my father used to say.

105
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:26,250
There. Ambrose.

106
00:00:26,250 --> 00:00:26,500
And the rest of it, which is the part that
is on the mailbox.

107
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:26,750
H, for Halloran.

108
00:00:26,750 --> 00:00:27,000
My father drove a milk truck and my mother
took in sewing.

109
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:27,250
Four of us out of a three-room flat, and
all four of us fed.

110
00:00:27,250 --> 00:00:27,500
I have written this name on thirty-four
years of forms.

111
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:27,750
And I have carried it up and down these
same streets for all of them.

112
00:00:27,750 --> 00:00:28,000
Nearly done.

113
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:28,250
The last of it now.

114
00:00:28,250 --> 00:00:28,500
Ambrose Halloran. That is who is sitting
at this table.

115
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:28,750
Ambrose Halloran, sixty-one, letter
carrier. That is the whole of the record.

116
00:00:28,750 --> 00:00:29,000
I am done. What is left over I am not
going to spend pretending I am stronger.

117
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:29,250
Discard it. A man my age does not get to
buy his youth back at a desk.

118
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:29,500
Yes. I have looked at myself long enough.
Now the door.

119
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:29,750
Before the door, before anything: stand
still and look at the room.

120
00:00:29,750 --> 00:00:30,000
There is a bench of another man things at
my back. I want something in my hands.

121
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:30,250
Straight down. Let me see what this man
kept on his bench.

122
00:00:30,250 --> 00:00:32,250
A welder hood. Heavy, and I will see
almost nothing out of it.

123
00:00:32,250 --> 00:00:32,500
But it is my whole face inside a shell,
and being bitten is what I fear.

124
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:32,750
Look at the watch, then look at the
street. That is the only rule I keep.

125
00:00:32,750 --> 00:00:33,000
No. Something out there is getting to its
feet and I am stood in an open bay.

126
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:33,250
West, along the bench, away from the
daylight.

127
00:00:33,250 --> 00:00:33,500
Quietly. Whatever else I am, I am good at
quietly.

128
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:34,500
Five of them, out east past the drive. I
have seen them. Tell me about a sixth.

129
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:35,500
West, along the bench, out of the daylight
and the open bay.

130
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:36,500
Quietly. Whatever else I am, I am good at
quietly.

131
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:36,750
Past the extinguisher on the wall. I
noticed it, which is what I do.

132
00:00:36,750 --> 00:00:37,750
There is a door at this end. I have posted
mail through this house a hundred times.

133
00:00:37,750 --> 00:00:38,750
From the outside, always. Never once from
in here.

134
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:39,750
The bay behind me stands wide open. A door
is what I want between us.

135
00:00:39,750 --> 00:00:40,750
Two paces more and it is on my right hand.

136
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:41,000
Through it, and then shut it. That is my
whole plan for this minute.

137
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:41,250
No window and one door. It will do. A man
could be worse placed.

138
00:00:41,250 --> 00:00:41,500
So I wait, and I listen. That is all today
is going to be.

139
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:51,500
Three hours. I will give the street three
hours and then look again.

140
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:52,750
Glass. And that is glass going in, not
glass falling. Something is inside.

141
00:00:52,750 --> 00:00:53,750
There is a fire extinguisher on the floor
at my boots. I want it in my hands.

142
00:00:53,750 --> 00:00:54,000
Here. Under my own feet, where I have been
stood this whole time.

143
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:54,250
Nothing at my feet after all. Through the
door then, and away from that noise.

144
00:00:54,250 --> 00:00:54,500
And shut it. A shut door has saved more
people than courage ever has.

145
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:54,750
Behind me, south. Pull it to and let it
hold.

146
00:00:54,750 --> 00:00:55,000
Seven paces. I have heard it, and I will
not stand here counting.

147
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,000
North, through the door, into the dark
part of the house.

148
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:57,250
Never mind the door for a second. I am
stood in the middle of it.

149
00:00:57,250 --> 00:00:57,500
I can see it now. Out on the grass past
the bay, and it is coming this way.

150
00:00:57,500 --> 00:00:57,750
North. Off the threshold, so the door has
somewhere to swing to.

151
00:00:57,750 --> 00:00:58,000
Yes. I know they are out there. Stop
stopping me and let me walk.

152
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:58,250
North. Off the threshold, so the door has
somewhere to swing to.

153
00:00:58,250 --> 00:00:58,500
Now the door.

154
00:00:58,500 --> 00:00:59,500
South, behind me. Pull it to.

155
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:00,500
Move anyway. Standing still in a doorway
is exactly how a man gets caught in one.

156
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:00,750
North. Away from the bay and the noise,
into the house proper.

157
00:01:00,750 --> 00:01:01,000
A house has a stair in it, and a stair has
a bedroom at the top.

158
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:01,250
East, then. The house is that way and the
noise is behind a shut door.

159
00:01:01,250 --> 00:01:01,500
One thing done properly this morning. That
door is closed and I closed it.

160
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:01,750
Looking for a stair. Up is always better
than along.

161
00:01:01,750 --> 00:01:02,000
Then I stay. One door, no window, and a
tank of clean water. It will do.

162
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:02,250
Let the day go past me, instead of me
going past it.

163
00:01:02,250 --> 00:01:06,250
Three hours. Then I look at the watch and
think again.

164
00:01:06,250 --> 00:01:06,500
Crunching. That is a foot on broken glass,
and it is indoors with me.

165
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:06,750
Nothing I can see from in here, and
nothing in here that can see me. Good.

166
00:01:06,750 --> 00:01:07,000
So I sit down with my back against the
machine and wait it out.

167
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Three hours again. I have waited longer
than that for a bus.

168
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:17,000
I am not going to jump at every noise. A
man who does that never rests at all.

169
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:17,250
Close. Right. Up on my feet, then, and no
dawdling about it.

170
00:01:17,250 --> 00:01:17,500
Two paces off, with a door between us. I
go the other way and I go now.

171
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:17,750
North into the dark. Anything at all but
the direction it is coming from.

172
00:01:17,750 --> 00:01:18,000
Wall. East then, and quickly, before it
comes round that frame.

173
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:18,250
Cornered in a bathroom at sixty-one.
Southwest, past the frame, and out.

174
00:01:18,250 --> 00:01:18,500
My legs have gone stupid on me. East.
Move.

175
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:18,750
Again. There is a tub in the way and I am
climbing out of it.

176
00:01:18,750 --> 00:01:19,750
Then there is one thing left to do, and it
is the thing I know how to do.

177
00:01:19,750 --> 00:01:20,000
Shut it. Shut it and be still and let the
thing lose interest in me.

178
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:20,250
It came through the door I shut. Nowhere
behind me. The hood, then. Now.

179
00:01:20,250 --> 00:01:20,500
It will not go on over my hat. Of course
it will not. Sixty-one years of that.

180
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:20,750
Then it goes in my hands. It is heavy, and
heavy is what I have not got.

181
00:01:20,750 --> 00:01:21,000
No. Put it back. Not one mark on me, and
it is stood right there.

182
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:21,250
It is dark in here and I have not moved.
It does not know I am here.

183
00:01:21,250 --> 00:01:21,500
So I do the hardest thing I have ever done
in my life, which is nothing.

184
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:21,750
Twenty seconds. Then I look. Then twenty
more.

185
00:01:21,750 --> 00:01:22,000
It grabbed at me. It knows. Up, up, stop
pretending to be furniture.

186
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Then fists. I am sixty-one and I have
never hit a living thing in my life.

187
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:24,250
Yes. Stop. It is right on me and I have
been sitting here counting.

188
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:24,500
Then fists. I am sixty-one and I have
never hit a living thing in my life.

189
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:24,750
Nothing. I kicked it and it did not even
notice. My hands are shaking.

190
00:01:24,750 --> 00:01:25,000
Not the whistle.

191
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:25,250
Not the telephone.

192
00:01:25,250 --> 00:01:25,500
Not the wallet, for the love of God.

193
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:25,750
Not the letters. Somebody mother wrote one
of those.

194
00:01:25,750 --> 00:01:28,750
There. Under everything, the way it always
is.

195
00:01:28,750 --> 00:01:29,750
Both hands. It weighs like a bowling ball
and that is the whole point.

196
00:01:29,750 --> 00:01:31,750
Both hands, over my head, down. The way
you split kindling in a cold shed.

197
00:01:31,750 --> 00:01:32,000
The lavatory. A man can stand on a
lavatory. Nobody here to see me do it.

198
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:32,250
Again. Over it. Sixty-one years old and
climbing a toilet to live.

199
00:01:32,250 --> 00:01:32,500
I know. I know it is there. Let me go past
it and out.

200
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:32,750
West. Out of the corner and along the
wall.

201
00:01:32,750 --> 00:01:33,000
Keep going. Do not turn round and look at
it.

202
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:33,250
Down into the corner. Come at me here and
leave that doorway empty.

203
00:01:33,250 --> 00:01:33,500
Yes. Seen. Noted. Now let my legs do what
I am telling them.

204
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:33,750
Down off the lavatory into the corner, and
let it follow me in.

205
00:01:33,750 --> 00:01:36,750
Noted. Again. Yes.

206
00:01:36,750 --> 00:01:37,000
Back onto the tub. If it comes at me here,
the door behind it opens up.

207
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:37,250
That patch by the cistern. I never looked
at it properly and I should have.

208
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:37,500
Up and to my left. Thirty-four years of
noticing and I missed one wall.

209
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:37,750
Too late to be curious. It has got its
teeth in me.

210
00:01:37,750 --> 00:01:38,000
Down on it again. It keeps falling over.
Perhaps I only need it to fall.

211
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:38,250
Enough. It has its teeth in my coat. I do
not need telling twice.

212
00:01:38,250 --> 00:01:38,500
I have been fiddling with the wrong thing.
Off. Leave me be and let me swing.

213
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:38,750
The warnings. Off. I can see perfectly
well what is in front of me.

214
00:01:38,750 --> 00:01:39,000
I have heard you. Now let me try behind me
once more.

215
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:39,250
North. Perhaps there is more room here
than I gave it credit for.

216
00:01:39,250 --> 00:01:39,500
Once more, and then east.

217
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:39,750
East. Anything. Any direction that is not
the one with teeth in it.

218
00:01:39,750 --> 00:01:40,000
Yes. I know. I have known since the glass
went in downstairs.

219
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:40,250
Down on it again. It is like hitting a
coat packed full of wet sand.

220
00:01:40,250 --> 00:01:40,500
Again.

221
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:40,750
My arms are going. Sixty-one years, and
this is what they are for.

222
00:01:40,750 --> 00:01:41,000
And again.

223
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:41,250
I am not going to be able to do this for
very long. I know that too.

224
00:01:41,250 --> 00:01:42,250
Not now. Not this. Shut it and look at
what has hold of me.

225
00:01:42,250 --> 00:01:43,250
It has my leg. That is why I could not
move. Pull, then. Pull.

226
00:01:43,250 --> 00:01:44,250
Pull. It has my trouser leg and my leg
inside it.

227
00:01:44,250 --> 00:01:45,250
Thirty-four years of walking. Pull, you
old fool, pull.

228
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:46,250
It is stronger than I am. Of course it is.
Everything is.

229
00:01:46,250 --> 00:01:47,250
My mother hauled a stuck calf out of a
ditch at sixty. Pull.

230
00:01:47,250 --> 00:01:48,250
Again.

231
00:01:48,250 --> 00:01:49,250
Again, and I will not stop asking my legs
until they answer.

232
00:01:49,250 --> 00:01:50,250
Again. It has not got both legs and it has
not got my arms.

233
00:01:50,250 --> 00:01:51,250
A grip is only a grip. It has to hold and
I only have to move.

234
00:01:51,250 --> 00:01:52,250
Somebody wrote a letter to this house and
I put it through that door.

235
00:01:52,250 --> 00:01:53,250
I am not dying in a stranger bathroom on a
Thursday.

236
00:01:53,250 --> 00:01:54,250
Maureen is eleven houses in on Elm with a
wind chime made of spoons.

237
00:01:54,250 --> 00:01:55,250
And she does not drive, and she will be
waiting, and she will be cross.

238
00:01:55,250 --> 00:01:56,250
Pull.

239
00:01:56,250 --> 00:01:57,250
Pull.

240
00:01:57,250 --> 00:01:58,250
My knees are screaming and I have never
cared less about my knees.

241
00:01:58,250 --> 00:01:59,250
Again. Again. Again.

242
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:00,250
My head is ringing. It got me across the
head and I did not see it come.

243
00:02:00,250 --> 00:02:01,250
Pull. The grip is on the cloth as much as
the leg.

244
00:02:01,250 --> 00:02:02,250
Trousers tear. Trousers have always torn.
Let them.

245
00:02:02,250 --> 00:02:02,500
I have hauled a full sack up Torrey Street
in February. Pull.

246
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:02,750
Again.

247
00:02:02,750 --> 00:02:03,000
It does not get tired. That is the unfair
part of it.

248
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:03,250
But it does keep falling over, and I am
still standing up.

249
00:02:03,250 --> 00:02:03,500
Pull.

250
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:03,750
One good tear is all I want. One.

251
00:02:03,750 --> 00:02:04,000
Again.

252
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:04,250
I can hear my own breath and it sounds
like an old man.

253
00:02:04,250 --> 00:02:04,500
It is an old man. Pull anyway.

254
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:04,750
Then I go through the wall. It is somebody
plasterboard and I am past caring.

255
00:02:04,750 --> 00:02:05,000
East. The rest of the house is on the
other side of that.

256
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Again. The hood is a poor hammer, but it
is a hammer.

257
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:06,250
Through.

258
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:07,250
Plaster. Paper. A stud somewhere behind it
that I will have to find.

259
00:02:07,250 --> 00:02:07,500
Again.

260
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:08,500
I am making a noise that will bring the
whole street down on me.

261
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:08,750
I do not care. Through.

262
00:02:08,750 --> 00:02:09,750
This is what thirty-four years of carrying
has left in my shoulders.

263
00:02:09,750 --> 00:02:10,000
All of it, into this.

264
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Again.

265
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:11,250
Again.

266
00:02:11,250 --> 00:02:12,250
I cannot get out of its way any more. So I
stop trying and I just swing.

267
00:02:12,250 --> 00:02:12,500
Through.

268
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:13,500
It is hitting me, I am hitting the wall,
and one of us finishes first.

269
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:13,750
Again.

270
00:02:13,750 --> 00:02:14,750
There is daylight on the other side of
this. I have seen the windows.

271
00:02:14,750 --> 00:02:15,000
Again.

272
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Every letter I ever put through a door
went through a gap somebody made.

273
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:16,250
So make a gap.

274
00:02:16,250 --> 00:02:17,250
Again.

275
00:02:17,250 --> 00:02:17,500
Again.

276
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:18,500
My arms are water. Swing anyway.

277
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:19,500
Swing anyway.

278
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:20,500
The wall is not going to give. So I turn
round and face the thing itself.

279
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:22,500
I am sorry, Maureen. I did try to come. I
want that written down somewhere.

280
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:23,500
Eleven houses in, blue door, the chime she
made out of spoons.

281
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:24,500
Swing.

282
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:26,500
It is going to be quick and it is going to
be in a stranger bathroom.

283
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:27,500
Thirty-four years and I never once left a
route unfinished.

284
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:29,500
It does not hurt as much as I expected.
That is probably not a good sign.

285
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:31,500
Keep swinging. It is the only thing left
that is mine to decide.

286
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:32,500
Somebody will find this house. Somebody
always finds the house.

287
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:34,500
And there will be a hat on the floor of a
bathroom and they will wonder.

288
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:36,500
Ambrose Halloran. Sixty-one. Three routes,
all of them inside two miles.

289
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:39,500
I knew every bad step on every porch on
this street.

290
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:41,500
That is not nothing. A man can be made of
smaller things than that.

291
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:44,500
Swing.

292
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:45,500
My head has gone light and the room is
going long at the edges.

293
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:48,500
Swing.

294
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:50,500
I am not frightened, which surprises me
more than anything today.

295
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:53,500
Neither snow nor rain, they told us.
Nobody said anything about this.

296
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:53,750
My left arm has stopped answering. I will
use the other one.

297
00:02:53,750 --> 00:02:54,000
Swing.

298
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:54,250
I would have liked to see the chime move.
That is all. That is the whole wish.

299
00:02:54,250 --> 00:02:54,500
Swing.

300
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:54,750
Tell her I was on my way. Tell her I was
only four days late.

301
00:02:54,750 --> 00:02:55,000
Swing.

302
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:55,250
Cold. Everything has gone cold and very
quiet.

303
00:02:55,250 --> 00:02:55,500
Swing.

304
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:55,750
One more.

305
00:02:55,750 --> 00:03:02,750
One more.

306
00:03:02,750 --> 00:03:08,750
Yes it is slow. Everything I do is slow.
Get on with it.

307
00:03:08,750 --> 00:03:14,750
Two. I hurt it. After all that, I actually
hurt it.

308
00:03:14,750 --> 00:03:21,750
It went through the glasses. I felt them
go.

309
00:03:21,750 --> 00:03:27,750
Again.

310
00:03:27,750 --> 00:03:34,750
Again.

311
00:03:34,750 --> 00:03:35,750
I cannot see out of one side now.

312
00:03:35,750 --> 00:03:36,000
Again.

313
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:36,250
My hand went again. Nothing left to hit,
and the 2 landed in my last words.

314
00:03:36,250 --> 00:03:36,500
That is not what I said. The 2 would not
come off, so I wrote round it.

315
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:36,750
O.

316
00:03:36,750 --> 00:03:37,000
On.

317
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:37,250
On. Then a space, and the rest of it.

318
00:03:37,250 --> 00:03:37,500
It is not much of a sentence but it is a
true one.

319
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:37,750
On my.

320
00:03:37,750 --> 00:03:38,000
That is what I was. Four days late and on
my way.

321
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:38,250
W.

322
00:03:38,250 --> 00:03:38,500
Nearly said.

323
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:38,750
On my way.

324
00:03:38,750 --> 00:03:39,000
Full stop. Thirty-four years of straight
addresses, and a 2 on the last one.

325
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:39,250
Let it stand. That is the last thing I
have to file.

326
00:03:39,250 --> 00:03:39,500
No. I was there for it. Once was enough
for any man.
